---
import Content from "@/layouts/Content.astro";
import AdSlot from "@/components/site/AdSlot.astro";
import { WORD_LISTS, listWords } from "@/engine/decks/wordlists";

/**
 * Word Jungle's front door — the organic-search surface for the word decks, in
 * the same shape as the times-table pages: real prerendered HTML that answers
 * the query itself, with the game one link away.
 *
 * The page a parent reads; `/spelling/play` next door is the game itself.
 * Splitting them is what lets this one answer the search — "sight words" is a
 * different query from "times tables", and the answer to it is prose a crawler
 * can read rather than an island it can't.
 */

const title =
  "Sight words and spelling practice — free Dolch word lists | School Skills";
const description =
  "The Dolch sight words, graded by age, as a free spelling game. Every word is read aloud in a sentence, so their/there and to/too/two are telling apart rather than guessing. No sign-up, works offline, progress stays on your device.";
---

<Content
  title={title}
  description={description}
  world="jungle"
  jsonLd={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "LearningResource",
    name: "Sight words and spelling practice",
    description,
    url: "https://schoolskills.app/spelling",
    learningResourceType: "Game",
    educationalLevel: "Elementary School",
    teaches: "Sight word recognition and spelling, Dolch word lists",
    typicalAgeRange: "4-9",
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    inLanguage: "en",
  }}
>
  <header class="hero wrap">
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">
      <span class="hero__world">Word Jungle</span>
      Spelling and sight words
    </p>
    <h1 class="hero__title">The words that don&rsquo;t play fair</h1>
    <p class="hero__lead">
      <em>Said</em>, <em>because</em>, <em>one</em> &mdash; the words a child can&rsquo;t
      sound their way to. Graded by age and drilled by ear, the way a spelling test
      actually works.
    </p>
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <a class="cta" href="/spelling/play">Start a spelling race</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero__note">Free &middot; no account &middot; works offline</p>
  </header>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">What sight words are</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most words can be sounded out. These can&rsquo;t, or not reliably: a
        child who attacks <em>said</em> phonetically arrives at &ldquo;say-id&rdquo;.
        Edward Dolch collected the worst offenders in 1936 and the list has barely
        moved since, because the words that break the rules are the common ones. Between
        them they account for something like half the words in a typical children&rsquo;s
        book, which is why they get memorised rather than decoded.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">The lists</h2>
    <p class="h2__sub">
      Graded by age rather than by school year, because Dolch&rsquo;s own grades
      are American and the mapping to other systems is approximate. Pick the one
      that matches the reader, not the one that matches the label.
    </p>
    <table class="facts">
      <caption>The Dolch sight words, by age</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">List</th>
          <th scope="col">Age</th>
          <th scope="col">Words</th>
          <th scope="col">Starts with</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          WORD_LISTS.map((list) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{list.name}</th>
              <td>{list.group}</td>
              <td>{list.entries.length}</td>
              <td>{listWords(list).slice(0, 4).join(", ")}&hellip;</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><AdSlot name="article" /></div>
  <section class="band band--inset">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="h2">Heard in a sentence, not copied</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A spelling card that shows you the word is a handwriting exercise. So
          the word is read out instead &mdash; and then read out again inside a
          short sentence, which is what a teacher does and what this list in
          particular needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          It needs it because these words collide.
          <em>Their</em>, <em>there</em> and <em>they&rsquo;re</em> are one sound;
          so are <em>to</em>, <em>too</em> and <em>two</em>,
          <em>one</em> and <em>won</em>, <em>ate</em> and <em>eight</em>,
          <em>no</em> and <em>know</em>. Asked to spell the sound on its own, a
          child cannot be wrong, because there is no answer. The commonest words
          are exactly the ones English has the most collisions in, which is why
          a sight-word list is dense with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The sentence is on screen as well as in the ear, with the word itself
          left as a blank &mdash; <em>&ldquo;This is ____ house&rdquo;</em>.
          That gives away which word is meant and nothing at all about how it is
          spelt. It also rescues the words a device&rsquo;s built-in voice
          mumbles: <em>sleep</em> is one soft consonant away from
          <em>sleeve</em> on several of them, and context repairs a mis-heard sound
          the way it always does.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing is recorded and nothing is sent anywhere &mdash; the voice is
          the one already installed on the device. Where there is no voice at
          all, the blank fills itself in for a moment and empties again, which
          is weaker than hearing it but still recall rather than transcription.
        </p>
        <p>
          Younger readers can switch to <em>spot it</em>: hear the word, read
          the sentence, and pick it out of four. Recognition first, spelling
          second.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">This week&rsquo;s spellings, as a deck</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The lists above are a starting point, not the point. Paste in whatever
        came home &mdash; a newline, a comma or a semicolon between them,
        however they arrived &mdash; and they become a deck like any other, with
        their own records and their own trouble list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Save one as a file and another family can open it and get their own
        copy. Everything stays on the device it was typed on; there is no
        account and nothing is uploaded, so a shared list travels the way a
        photocopy would &mdash; because someone sent it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Practising it properly</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Words that come out wrong, or come out slowly, collect in a trouble list
        you can drill on their own. A word only counts as known once it&rsquo;s
        right three times running and under four seconds &mdash; the same bar
        the times tables are held to, and for the same reason: recall, not
        working it out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Short and frequent beats long and rare. Ten words a day will move a list
        faster than a whole list once a week.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        This is spelling practice, not a reading programme. It won&rsquo;t teach
        phonics, it doesn&rsquo;t follow a curriculum, and it has no opinion
        about how you teach reading &mdash; it drills the list you give it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <a class="cta" href="/spelling/play">Start a spelling race</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Content>
